Introducing data fetching in the layouts is a really great feature, but it can complicate on-demand revalidation that was introduced in  v12.1.0. For example if the navigation data fetched in root layout changes, you will have to revalidate all the pages one by one. It would be helpful if you could pass a glob to the `res.unstable_revalidate` like this:
```ts
res.unstable_revalidate('/*');
res.unstable_revalidate('/dashboard/*');
```

Longer-term both approaches will be supported but currently Suspense for data fetching is not stable yet and not specifically tied to React Server Components, getting to the result where components are rendered server-side while data fetching happens in getServerSideProps/getStaticProps will be the intermediate state and longer term both will be supported.

The main benefit for fetching data outside of the component tree currently is that it does not create a render-fetch-render-fetch waterfall which is explained in this part of the RFC: https://nextjs.org/blog/layouts-rfc#parallel-fetching-and-rendering

The data fetching will be kicked off eagerly regardless of rendering as we know what layouts / page will be rendered before the React rendering starts.

This diagram from the blogpost explains it visually:

<img width="1798" alt="parallel" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6324199/170029407-78cbde63-d0be-40e7-ba34-8bfd5787f1e9.png">

The simplest demo of this would be:

```js
// app/dashboard/layout.js
export async function getServerSideProps() {
    // Simulate intentionally slow data fetching by 5 seconds
   await new Promise((resolve) => {
     setTimeout(resolve, 5000)
   })
   return {
      message: 'Dashboard'
   }
}

export default function Layout({message, children}) {
    return <>
      <h1>{message}</h1>
      {children}
    </>
}


// app/dashboard/teams/page.js
export async function getServerSideProps() {
   // Simulate intentionally slow data fetching by 2 seconds
   await new Promise((resolve) => {
     setTimeout(resolve, 2000)
   })
   return {
      message: 'Hello to the teams page'
   }
}

export default function Page({message}) {
    return <>
      <p>{message}</p>
    </>
}
```

With this example `getServerSideProps` for `app/dashboard/teams/page.js` will be finished before the rendering of `app/dashboard/layout.js` even starts as it takes 2 seconds to resolve whereas the data for the layout takes 5. Total render would take around 5 seconds.

If you were to fetch data during rendering of `Page` you'd have to wait 5 seconds, then rendering of Page starts which waits for the data fetching that takes another 2 seconds before the part of the page is there. Total render would take around 7 seconds.

As you can see the timing would grow as you add more data fetching, if you had another layout that took 3 seconds and then another that took 4 seconds you'd end up taking a total of 14 seconds compared to 5 seconds when the run in parallel.

Note: I'm intentionally using large numbers to clearly show the difference, in practice these numbers will be x milliseconds but as shown it's would stack up in a similar way.

I love this. Will component-level `Suspense` be able to be used interchangeably with `getServerSideProps` and will there be a way to get page params from within the component?

I.e. would these two examples below be the same thing?

**Example 1 with `getServerSideProps`**:


```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.js

export async function getServerSideProps({ params }) {
  const post = await getPostFromCMS(params.slug); 

  return {
    props: { post },
  };
}

export default function BlogPostPage({ post }) {
  return <Post post={post} />;
}
```

**Example 2 with suspense in render**:

> Assuming that `getPostFromCMS` works the same on the client and server or that there's a `useQuery()`-fn which is informed by a context provider.


```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.js
export default function BlogPostPage({ params }) {
  const post = getPostFromCMS(params.slug); // -> throw `Promise`
  return <Post post={post} />;
}
```

Should Layout components support named slots, in addition to `children` (`page.js`)? 

Looking at the `DashboardLayout` example from the blog post, it doesn't look like there would be a straightforward way for a route to override the contents of the `<Header>` or `<Footer>` because they're baked into a parent layout:

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3022244/170119836-69800b09-31d0-4887-9942-3986daa87902.png)

Named slots could allow more flexibility to override parts of a shared layout, while still only re-rendering the bits that changed on each navigation. E.g:

```jsx
// Root layout (app/layout.js)
// - Applies to all routes
export default function RootLayout({ children, header }) {
  return (
    <html>
      <body>
        {header}
        {children}
        <Footer />
      </body>
    </html>
  )
}

// Named slot, default value (app/header.js)
export default function Header() {
  return <header>...</header>
}

// Regular layout (app/dashboard/layout.js)
export default function DashboardLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <>
      <DashboardSidebar />
      {children}
    </>
  )
}

// Page Component (app/dashboard/analytics/page.js)
export default function AnalyticsPage() {
  return (
    <main>...</main>
  )
}

// Named slot, override (app/dashboard/analytics/header.js)
export default function AnalyticsHeader() {
  return <header>Analytics Header</header>
}

```

Technically there will be something like this in the follow up RFC regarding parallel routes, but it's not actually for this use case (and ergonomically is not for this use case).

For this type of shared layouts, it's often better not to share the state of the common parts between pages. 

You can still have a shared layout (or template if you will) by making a reused component.

```js
// Root layout (app/root-template.js)

function Header() {
  return <header>...</header>
}

export default function RootTemplate({ children, header }) {
  return (
    <html>
      <body>
        {header || <Header />}
        {children}
        <Footer />
      </body>
    </html>
  )
}
```

```js
// Page Component (app/dashboard/analytics/page.js)

import {Template} from '../../root-template.js';

function AnalyticsHeader() {
  return <header>Analytics Header</header>
}

export default function AnalyticsPage() {
  return (
    <Template header={<AnalyticsHeader />}>
      <main>...</main>
    </Template>
  )
}
```

These have slightly different semantics because this way of composing them would create two separate stateful instances of each shared component which can affect things like animations.

Depending on your layout one or the other is preferred. I suspect for this kind of reuse it's most often preferred to use the one with separate state like this pattern above.

You'll see in the follow up that the other way is still possible but it's not as ergonomic (it's more ergonomic for parallel or conditional routing). If it turns out that this use case is often needed with shared state then we could also include a way to do that more ergonomically.

We're currently using React Router and looking into migrating to Next for a while. Nested Routes will definitely make this easier, but we heavily pass data from parent route segments to child route segments as well. Is it allowed to have a function as `children`, so we can do something like this?

```
// customized example from the blog
// app/blog/layout.js
export async function getStaticProps() {
  const categories = await getCategoriesFromCMS();

  return {
    props: { categories },
  };
}

export default function BlogLayout({ categories, children }) {
  return (
    <>
      <BlogSidebar categories={categories} />
      {children({ categories })}
    </>
  );
}
```

I'd like to avoid Context, because it increases the indirection and is more verbose. It would of course also be nice if TypeScript can properly check this (e.g. `children` which is passed to `BlogLayout` must be a function which accepts `{ categories }`).

