Hey y'all! This RFC seems really exciting and I look forward to using it! Somewhat related to [this comment here](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/37136#discussioncomment-2807934) I think passing some sort of `<Page>` or `<Layout>` component could allow for more flexible layouts. For example, something like this:

```ts
export function Layout({Layout, children}) {
  return (
    <SomeReactContextProvider>
      <SomeSidebarUsingContext />
      <Layout>
        {children}
      </Layout>
    </SomeReactContextProvider>
  )
}
```

I could see this being useful for creating something like "debug" views of a page, where info can be embedded in a UI chrome that surrounds the main page. (This isn't a perfect example, but similar to how something like [Cypress](https://www.cypress.io/) provides testing info for the page it's running on)

I don't think moving away from file based routing is in the spirit of Nextjs so I don't think that direction makes a lot of sense. Why this is seen as an either / or type thing I don't really get. There'll have to be some code that would traverse the hierarchy on disk and translate that to some sort of data-structure that works for Nextjs' router / renderer. It would be great if you could tap into the hierarchy building part programmatically from (for example) `next.config.js` or a `routes.js` or something. For example:

`routes.js`

```tsx

export default async (route) => {
   // set layout dashboard
  route('/dashboard/layout.js', import.meta.resolve('my-layouts/centered'))

  // add page route
  route('/dashboard/index.js', import.meta.resolve('my-pages/dashboard'))
}

```

This is just an example of course. I'm sure we could come up with something nicer.

The result of the function would be _merged_ with whatever hierarchy would be part of the `app` directory but is totally optional. Where this would really shine is in re-using page-module from somewhere other than the local repository / app directory. We manage over 14 different but similar websites which share a lot of code. This would make it much easier to integrate local custom pages with some page-modules from an npm package for example. It could also open the door to entire pages just imported from some external party which provides a Nextjs page module.

First, I'd like to say: good job with Layouts, it may help us with multiple problems related to nested routing. Still, I think it doesn't resolve the issue that concerns me the most: untyped, loosely defined routes using the file system.

I believe it would be crucial to utilize TypeScript in Next.js routing. File-based routing, though seemingly convenient, does not give as much of an idea on types. The situation with what we are able to include in our directories and what we are able to include in our URLs inside the app is very confusing.

Having a routing system compatible with TypeScript, we could avoid multiple bugs and simplify maintenance of a complex application.

To show what I mean, I've prepared an example. Let's imagine we would have a config file, e.g., `routing.ts`. In this file, we could define our routes and their parameters:

```ts
{
  "/": {
    component: HomePage
  },
  "/about": {
    component: AboutPage
  },
  "/products/:categoryId/:productId": {
    component: ProductPage
    searchParameters: ["page", "perPage"]
  },
}
```

We could harness TypeScript to extract the types from the routing definition. TypeScript's features, like template literal types and extend clause with infer, make it possible. As a result, we would get strongly-typed components and hooks to use across the app. `Link` would work like the following:

https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/32204867/187017953-b522f874-ac38-4c1b-90e8-dcee3be6e79a.mp4

`useRouter` equivalent:

https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/32204867/187017974-cc298652-ac54-41e3-8463-3815e929792a.mp4

Is it possible to implement something like that, beside the Layouts feature?

